<section>
    <h3>Adapt it to your API</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-yaml" data-trim data-line-numbers="1-8|3-4|5-6|7-8">
http_client:
    recorder:
        # headers, query string, forms and JSON: added to the built-in lists
        redact: ['X-Custom-Secret', 'sig', 'pin']
        # never redacted, removed from the built-in lists
        redact_except: ['code']
        # or replace the redaction entirely
        redactor: ~                 # RedactorInterface service
    </code></pre>
    <p class="fragment subtitle" style="font-size: 0.6em;">
        <code>redact_except</code>: when <code>code</code> is not a secret in your tests, and you need it.
    </p>
    <p class="fragment callout">
        A field that changes on every request (timestamp, nonce, request id)? Add it to <code>redact</code>:
        redacted on both sides, it is also <b>ignored when matching</b>.
    </p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>Your API has its own secrets? You can configure it.</p>
        <p>[click] redact adds names to the built-in lists.</p>
        <p>[click] redact_except removes names from them.</p>
        <p>[click] Or you write your own RedactorInterface service.</p>
        <p>[click] redact_except is useful when code, for example, is not a secret in your tests, and you need its value.</p>
        <p>[click] A tip: a value that changes every time, like a timestamp or a request id? Add it to redact. It's redacted on both sides, so it's also ignored when we compare requests.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
